  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x|\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}-2$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2-{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/9fade66030e2e416f92138d787999d3ca3a7dd4788ad39cf29dd07ff944038da.png)",
